The protocol SDK
================

Phase 6 of doc/proposals/006-comunicaciones-unificadas.md (§7.6).  It
lives in sdk/, it is TypeScript, and it is the client half of everything
the server grew: message tags, batches, labeled responses, SASL, multiline,
history, read markers, redaction, editing, search, rich text.

The point of writing it at all is that this server's additions are only
worth having if a client can use them without reading twelve
specifications and this file's worth of decisions.  The point of writing
it *here*, in the same repository, is that the wire is one thing: a
change to ircd/msg_tag.c and a change to sdk/packages/protocol are the
same change, and when they are not, the live test below says so.


WHAT IS IN IT
=============

Two packages, and the split between them is the one the server makes
between ircd/sasl.c and ircd/m_authenticate.c, or ircd/migration.c and
ircd/migration_run.c:

  @blacknode/irc-protocol   The wire, and nothing else.  Parse a line,
                            build a line, and the state machines that sit
                            on top of it: capability negotiation, SASL,
                            batches, the conversation tags.  No sockets,
                            no timers, no globals, no dependencies.

  @blacknode/irc-client     A connection, the state it carries and the
                            conversation on top: `Client`, `NetworkState`,
                            the transport interface, and the worker that
                            makes many tabs one connection.

The half that decides things can then be tested exhaustively, and it is
the half where being wrong is expensive.


WHAT IT RUNS ON
===============

Three targets, named in the roadmap, and one transport interface with
four methods between them and everything else:

  web        Next.js on Bun.  `WebSocketTransport`, normally inside the
             SharedWorker (below), so that ten tabs are one connection.
  mobile     React Native.  `WebSocketTransport` directly: there is one
             JavaScript context, so there is nothing to share between.
  desktop    Wails.  The frontend is a webview, so it is the browser's
             WebSocket and the web code runs unchanged.

Nothing in either package imports `window`, `document`, `process`,
`Buffer` or the DOM types.  What it needs from the runtime is declared,
in one file -- packages/protocol/src/globals.d.ts -- and it is
`TextEncoder`, `TextDecoder`, `setTimeout` and `clearTimeout`.  That file
is the portability contract: anything that would have to be added to it
is a decision, not a convenience.  It is why base64 is twenty lines of
our own rather than `btoa` (Latin-1 only) or `Buffer` (not in a browser),
and why the tsconfig does not include the DOM library.


A FRAME IS NOT A STREAM
=======================

The one thing IRC over WebSocket changes about reading the protocol.
The `text.ircv3.net` subprotocol -- which ircd/websocket.c speaks -- puts
**one IRC message in one frame, with no trailing CR LF**, and a
fragmented WebSocket message is reassembled before the client sees it.
So a transport that buffers until it sees a newline, which is the right
thing over TCP, never delivers a single line and looks exactly like a
server that never answered.  `WebSocketTransport` treats a frame as a
message, splits it on newlines anyway (a server is allowed to put several
in one frame, and some send the terminator), and holds nothing between
frames.


THE CONNECTION, AND HOW MANY OF THEM
====================================

    import { Client, WebSocketTransport } from '@blacknode/irc-client';

    const client = new Client({
      transport: new WebSocketTransport({ url: 'wss://irc.example.net/' }),
      nick: 'maria',
      credential: { mechanism: 'PLAIN', address: 'maria@example.com',
                    password: '...' },
    });

    client.on((event) => { /* a closed union of events */ });
    client.connect();

Registration is CAP first, SASL before CAP END, and the client waits for
903 before ending negotiation: the exchange is relayed to the network's
services and CAP END is what lets registration finish
(doc/readme.accounting).

In a browser that connection belongs in a worker:

    // app/irc.worker.ts
    import '@blacknode/irc-client/worker/shared-worker';

    // in a tab
    const worker = new SharedWorker(new URL('./irc.worker.ts',
                                            import.meta.url),
                                    { type: 'module' });
    worker.port.start();

`WorkerHub` is all of the decisions and none of the platform, which is
what lets it be tested with a fake port.  Three of those decisions are
worth stating:

  - A tab that just opened is sent a **snapshot, not a replay**.  Three
    hours of events is not what a tab opening into a conversation wants;
    what is on the screen is.
  - Closing one tab does not close the connection.  A person who closed
    one of eight has not gone offline.
  - A message that deserves a notification raises **one**, in one tab,
    preferring a hidden one.  Eight tabs each raising the same
    notification is eight notifications for one message.

Mentions are matched by hand rather than with a regular expression,
because a nickname may contain [ ] \ ^ { } | -- several of which are
regular-expression syntax -- and folded with the server's casemapping,
where [ ] \ are the capitals of { } |.


AN ACCOUNT IS NOT A NICKNAME
===========================

The network's services keep the accounts; the nickname is what somebody
happens to be wearing.  So a `User` has both: `account` (the name they
are logged in to, when `identified` is true) and `nick`, and a nick
change leaves the account exactly where it was.  `frozen` (umode +f) is
the services holding somebody still while they work out whether the
nickname is theirs.

An account reaches this SDK three ways, and all three land in the same
field: `+r <account>` in a MODE, `ACCOUNT <account>` (account-notify),
and the account parameter of a JOIN (extended-join).  Both capabilities
are in `WANTED_CAPS`; without them an account is only ever learnt by
asking, which this SDK does not do for you.


TESTS
=====

    cd sdk && bun install
    bun test              # everything; no server, no network
    bun run typecheck     # strict, exactOptionalPropertyTypes,
                          # noUncheckedIndexedAccess

and, against a real server:

    bun packages/client/test/live.ts ws://127.0.0.1:7799/

The live test is not part of `bun test` on purpose: it needs an ircd with
a WebSocket port.  Run it when the wire changes, which is the only time a
fake transport cannot tell you anything -- it is what found both halves
of the multiline bug (a client that never delivered a line, and a server
that truncated one), neither of which any unit test could have seen.

A server for it is three lines in a config:

    Port { port = 7799; websocket = yes; };

plus NETWORK_FEATURES and NETWORK_TIME on, which is what carries msgid
and the message's time.
